it's a voyage that began only a few  weeks ago in southern mexico  but for seven members of mexico's  zapatista army of national liberation  it's a journey 500 years in the making  after traveling one thousand kilometers  over land they prepared a small flotilla  of canoes for a transatlantic voyage  the delegation is codenamed squadron 421  and their mission is a symbolic conquest  of europe  we want to learn more how these other  worlds are we want to listen we want to  share  we want to find out how it is so we can  learn  in the mexican capital supporters of the  militant socialist collective known  as the zapatistas gathered to wish safe  travels to the would-be conquerors  this is a trip that has its first stop  in europe and includes a visit to 30  countries  the intention is to also arrive in other  continents  after leading a brief armed rebellion in  1994 in southern mexico  the zapatistas have often been  characterized as a militant  separatist group though the collective  has since evolved into more of a  political organization that places an  emphasis  on the rights of indigenous people  activists say they hope demonstrations  like these will dispel rumors about the  zapatista rebel collective  and teach more people about the history  of mexico's revolutionary movements  organizers here in mexico city  encouraged demonstrators  to build their own paper boats a gesture  that both symbolizes the first arrival  of spanish conquistadors and the  returned voyage by sapatista activists  now five centuries later  as the seven member armada of sapatista  conquistadors  prepared to set sail for the old world  they were cheered on by indigenous  activists  they are in the fight in the resistance  and so are we  we believe a different world is possible  [Music]  event organizers say beyond the symbolic  conquest  they hope this action will help inspire  global awareness for environmental and  social  progress through a more open dialogue  between cultures  manuel 